Finding yourself atop a hill east of the great ruined temple, you look around 
at the ancient solarium which overlooks the great eastern sea. Far below where
you now stand, the cliffs descend to the crashing waves. Your height gives you
an uncommonly good perspective from which to observe the surrounding 
environment. You find that looking forward out this great solarium, you can 
actually see the infinite possibility of time like unto a fabric stretched out
over the expansive seas. The paths of mortals appear as threads of this fabric,
weaving a fractaline tapestry of infinite complexity into infinity. This 
tapestry centers around the continent of O'len, the world that is. Many bright
threads cross this continent into the future. Despite the infinity of 
possibilities, you notice that only two discrete images of O'len exist on the
horizon. One features a thriving metropolis, devoid of natural life, with the
populus seemingly united under a powerful ruler, the brightest thread emerging
from a convergence of several bright threads. The other seems simple and
vibrant, like an uncarved block of wood, with many diverse threads interwoven
with the ebb and flow of time. Nature thrives in the latter, while the former
is ruled by technology and industry. Beyond these images on the horizon, the 
future becomes fractaline and too complex to observe from your current vantage
point. 
